# How the methods work, in plain words

## Ns: how much flavor do a recipe's ingredients share?
Every ingredient has a set of flavor molecules in FlavorDB. For two ingredients we count the molecules they share. A recipe's **Ns** is the average of that count over all its ingredient pairs: Ns(R) = 2/(n(n−1)) Σ_{i<j} |C_i ∩ C_j| (Ahn et al. 2011; Jain, Rakhi & Bagler 2015, eq. 2). A cuisine's Ns is the mean over its recipes.
{% if net %}
*Worked example (slide 6).* Korea's five signature ingredients are {{ join_and(net.nodes) }}. The ten pairs share {{ net.edges | map(attribute=2) | join(", ") }} compounds, so their Ns is {{ f(net.ns, 1) }}. Soybean Sauce has only {{ net.sizes["Soybean Sauce"] }} flavor molecules, so every pair it is in shares very few — which is exactly why Soybean Sauce is Korea's top contributor to its contrasting pairing.
