## Z: more or less sharing than chance?
We build a **null cuisine** of {{ n0(M.n_rand) }} random recipes from the same cuisine's ingredients and recipe sizes, and compare: Z = √N_rand · (mean Ns_cuisine − mean Ns_null) / σ_null, where σ_null is the standard deviation of recipe-level Ns in the null. **Z > 0 = uniform pairing** (ingredients share more than chance, e.g. Italy Z = {{ f(R.z.Italy, 1) }}); **Z < 0 = contrasting** (e.g. Korea Z = {{ f(R.z.Korea, 1) }}).
Important: because σ_null is the spread of single recipes (not of the mean), the cuisine's size never enters; only N_rand does. So **Z = √N_rand × d = {{ f(M.sqrt_n_rand, 1) }} × d**, an effect size, not a significance test. We always also report d and compare at equal size.

## The four null models
All four keep the cuisine's own ingredient set and recipe-size distribution (Singh & Bagler 2018):
1. **Random**: ingredients drawn uniformly. Asks: is there any pairing pattern at all?
2. **Frequency**: drawn in proportion to how often the cuisine uses them. Asks: is the pattern just popularity? (In Fig. 4 this null brings Z close to 0.)
3. **Category**: each recipe's category mix is kept (e.g. 2 spices, 1 meat), slots filled at random. Asks: is it just the category structure?
4. **Frequency + category**: both constraints.

## Why we compare at equal size
The null draws from the sample's own ingredient pool. A small sample loses the rare ingredients, which share few compounds, so its null scores higher and ΔNs falls. USA at {{ E.n_sub }} recipes: ΔNs {{ f(E.usa.delta_ns_own_pool_n160) }} against its own pool, {{ f(E.usa.delta_ns_full_pool_n160) }} against the full pool. The naive sign rule fires on {{ pct(E.fpr) }} of genuine {{ E.n_sub }}-recipe samples. So each LLM cuisine ({{ F.design.recipes_per_region }} recipes per region) is placed in the distribution of {{ n0(E.n_genuine_per_region) }} genuine samples of the same region and size: too uniform above the 97.5% quantile, too contrasting below 2.5%, BH-corrected across regions.

## The other fingerprint layers
- **Flavor drivers (χ%)**: the % change in the cuisine's mean Ns when one ingredient is removed from its recipes (the definition we calibrated against the published top contributors).
- **Category fingerprint**: randomise only one category at a time and measure ΔNs (Jain, Rakhi & Bagler 2015); the vector over categories is compared by Spearman ρ.
- **Signature ingredients and SAI**: signature = top-10 by relative prevalence (P_ic minus its mean in other cuisines; Jain & Bagler 2018). SAI = how often an LLM uses them / how often real recipes do (1 = same).
- **Co-occurrence networks**: NPMI of ingredient pairs; we compare the top-100 pairs (Jaccard) and disparity-filter backbones.

## How the fidelity scorer works (D3)
Three parts, each standardised against genuine recipes of the cuisine and averaged with equal weights (chosen on validation):
1. **Classifier**: a {{ SC.classifier_name }} on ingredients and categories, temperature-calibrated (ECE {{ f(SC.ece_raw) }} → {{ f(SC.ece_calibrated) }}); test macro-F1 {{ f(SC.test_macro_f1) }} over {{ F.regions.n }} classes (chance {{ f(SC.chance, 3) }}), top-3 {{ f(SC.test_top3) }}. Linear so that its weights name the atypical ingredients for feedback.
2. **Co-occurrence**: the mean shrinkage-smoothed PMI of the recipe's ingredient pairs within the cuisine.
3. **Ns consistency**: 1 − |2F(Ns) − 1|, i.e. 1 at the cuisine's median Ns and 0 at the tails.

It separates real held-out recipes from other-cuisine / copy-mutate / null recipes with AUC {{ f(SC.auc_test.other_cuisines.composite) }} / {{ f(SC.auc_test.copy_mutate.composite) }} / {{ f(SC.auc_test.random_null.composite) }}. **Best-of-N** keeps the best-scoring admissible recipe out of N; **critique-and-revise** sends the scorer's feedback (atypical ingredients, missing signature ingredients that pair well, Ns too high or low) back to the model and asks for a revision of the same dish. Near-copies (Jaccard ≥ {{ M.near_copy }} to any CulinaryDB recipe) are never selected, and evaluation uses only held-out metrics (an independent gradient-boosting classifier on a disjoint split, JSD, SAI, fingerprint, diversity, novelty).
